{"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/cycles\/graphprotocols\/graph\/cyclebasis()"]}],"metadata":{"extendedModule":"GraphProtocols","modules":[{"relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"],"name":"Cycles"}],"role":"symbol","symbolKind":"method","remoteSource":{"fileName":"UndirectedCycles.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/Cycles\/UndirectedCycles.swift#L72"},"roleHeading":"Instance Method","externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6CyclesE10cycleBasisSay5Walks5CycleVy6VertexQz5Edges_5IndexQZGGyF","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleBasis","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:5Walks5CycleV","text":"Cycle","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa","text":"Edges","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa","text":"Index","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">]","kind":"text"}],"title":"cycleBasis()"},"abstract":[{"text":"A cycle basis: the fundamental cycles of the breadth-first spanning forest, one for each","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge outside the forest, in ascending position of that edge, so ","type":"text"},{"code":"edgeCount - vertexCount + c","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" cycles for c components. Roots are taken in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order and edges in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidentEdges","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order; a vertex’s tree edge is the one that discovered it. Self-loops and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"parallel edges are supported: a loop is a cycle of its own, and a second copy of an edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"closes a cycle of length 2.","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Cycles\/documentation\/Cycles\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/cycleBasis()"},"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Cycles\/documentation\/Cycles","doc:\/\/Cycles\/documentation\/Cycles\/GraphProtocols","doc:\/\/Cycles\/documentation\/Cycles\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleBasis","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Walks\/documentation\/Walks\/Cycle","text":"Cycle","preciseIdentifier":"s:5Walks5CycleV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex","text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Edges-swift.associatedtype","text":"Edges","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P5EdgesQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Index","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">]","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every cycle in the basis has exactly one edge outside the forest, so the cycles are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"independent, and every cycle of the graph is a sum of them (mod 2). Each starts at its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least vertex in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" order and leaves it through the lesser of its two edges there.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"O(n + m) plus the total length of the cycles.","type":"text"}]}]}],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph/Edges-swift.associatedtype":{"title":"Edges","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/graph\/edges-swift.associatedtype","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Edges-swift.associatedtype","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"associatedtype","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Edges","kind":"identifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Collection","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"role":"symbol","abstract":[]},"doc://Walks/documentation/Walks/Cycle":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Cycle","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A cycle: a closed path, at least one edge long, listed without repeating its start (NetworkX’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"simple_cycles"},{"type":"text","text":", petgraph). Vertex i goes to vertex (i + 1) mod "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"count"},{"type":"text","text":" through edge i, and no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex or edge position appears twice; a self-loop is a cycle of length 1, two parallel edges"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one of length 2. It is the witness cycle detection returns."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Cycle","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/walks\/cycle","kind":"symbol","title":"Cycle","identifier":"doc:\/\/Walks\/documentation\/Walks\/Cycle"},"doc://Cycles/documentation/Cycles":{"abstract":[],"role":"col